Another source of stimuli-driven molecular materials results from the construction of crystalline coordination frameworks formed by molecular units capable of undergoing a motion in the solid state. The structural versatility of coordination chemistry has allowed the engineering of materials having novel topologies and remarkable properties. One can, for example, introduce flexibility into solids by taking advantage of the structural flexibility of the ligand,15–17 or from the ability of the coordination site to accept, release or exchange coordinating molecules. These processes can induce structural changes in the coordination framework. The result will be a dynamic material that undergoes reversible structural changes to respond to the applied stimulus.18 However, these dynamic materials are not restricted to the use of flexible ligands or changes in the coordination sphere. Thus, one can induce a change in the electronic/magnetic properties of a system by inducing a controlled movement (a rotation, for example) of an asymmetric bridging ligand connecting two different metal sites, as that may cause a change in the crystal field around these coordination sites and, hence, in their electronic configurations (from high to low spin, for example).19
In this review we will focus on these extended crystalline materials to illustrate with different examples the various possibilities provided by coordination chemistry to design responsive dynamic materials. In particular, we will concentrate on those materials having magnetism as the tunable property with chemical and physical stimuli. We will present the effects on the magnetic properties of chemi- or physisorption of molecules as chemical stimuli, and light, pressure or temperature as physical stimuli, currently topics of great interest in molecular magnetism. So far, the research in this area has been mainly focused on the synthesis of new multifunctional materials combining magnetism with a second property (electrical conductivity or superconductivity, molecular bistablity,. . .).20–25 However, a more recent application of these materials concerns the nascent area of molecular spintronics,26,27 where responsive magnetic molecular materials, such as photomagnets,28 magneto-resistive materials,29 or multiferroic materials30,31 are very promising. However, all these magnetic materials are based on rigid lattices. Hence, they do not take advantage of the structural flexibility induced by the presence of either constituent or inserted molecules. A major source for these dynamic materials is provided by the so-called Metal–Organic Frameworks (MOFs). In general, these coordination polymers are composed of metal ions or metal clusters as nodes and multitopic organic ligands as linkers.32–36 They have potential applications that range from catalysis and separations of mixtures, to gas storage media for sensors and electronic devices.37–40 The most common strategy for the synthesis of MOFs involves the use of rigid ligands to bridge between the metal nodes leading to robust and rigid frameworks (static MOFs). However, as has been pointed above, there is growing interest in the search for dynamic MOFs. We will show in this review that magnetic MOFs are particularly sensitive to the structural changes provoked by an external stimulus.
The first part of this review presents dynamic magnetic MOFs which can be modified with a chemical stimulus, typically guest uptake or guest incorporation. The major part of this section deals with porous MOFs, where the presence of channels facilitates the entrance of the guest molecule. However, it is also shown that the presence of pores is not a requirement for chemisorption to take place. The second part of the review presents dynamic MOFs where a physical stimulus induces changes in the bridging ligands, and hence, in the magnetic properties. The examples here presented are intended to be crystal-to-crystal transformations, although it must be noted that some of these could also be a solvent mediated process, i.e. a microscopic recrystallization, analogous to that demonstrated for some anion exchange reactions involving crystalline solids.41,42
## 2. Chemically-driven dynamic MOFs
## 2.1. Porous MOFs
With the recent explosion in research on MOFs,43–56 the use of a chemical stimulus such as gas sorption to affect the magnetic properties of a porous coordination polymer has been extensively explored.9,57–60 The initial focus concentrated on the molecule-based magnets which show magnetic cooperativity at low temperatures. Still, this approach is not very practical for sensing applications as the sorption of molecules takes place at relatively high temperatures, while the magnetic ordering occurs at very low temperature. Spin-crossover complexes have been used to overcome this difficulty in combining magnetic cooperativity and porosity, as in this last case magnetic cooperativity is not needed. In fact, the spin-crossover phenomenon can occur near room temperature, being these paramagnetic complexes extremely sensitive to subtle structural changes in metal coordination environments.61–63 In the following sections we will illustrate with some relevant examples the possibilities provided by these two approaches.
2.1.1. Porous magnets. The sorption/desorption of guest molecules in porous magnets can be used to tune the magnetism due to the sensitivity of the exchange interactions to subtle structural changes. These structural changes can involve either the bridging ligands or, directly, the coordination environment around the metal ions. Dehydration processes have long been used to change the coordination environment and the dimensionality of a hydrated coordination polymer, thus provoking significant changes in the magnetic properties.64,65 However, this process is often irreversible or produces a collapse in the structure when the solvent is removed.66,67 Reversibility of such structural changes has been successfully achieved only with the use of MOFs. We should mention in this context Kahn’s pioneering work on ‘‘molecular magnetic sponges’’, where changes in the magnetic properties occurred upon sorption of water.68 However, the lack of structural data after the transformation limits the information on the changes that take place in both the metal centres and the ligands. This situation is similar in other recent examples of chiral magnets with important changes in the magnetic properties (a shift of $21\mathrm{~K~}$ in $T_{\mathrm{C}}\mathbf{\Psi}_{\mathbf{C}}$ ) upon reversible dehydration.69
Changes affecting the ligand. Small changes in the geometry of the bridging ligand are sufficient to affect the magnetic properties of a MOF. An example of this kind has been reported by Miyasaka and co-workers70 in the charge-transfer compound $[\{\mathrm{Ru}_{2}(\mathrm{O}_{2}\mathrm{CPh}\mathrm{-}O\mathrm{-}\mathrm{Cl})_{4}\}_{2}\mathrm{TCNQ}(\mathrm{MeO})_{2}]\cdot\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ $\left(o\mathrm{-ClPhCO_{2}}^{-}\right.$ $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}$ $o$ -chlorobenzoate; $\mathrm{TCNQ}(\mathrm{MeO})_{2}~=~2{,}5$ -dimethoxy-7,7,8,8-tetracyanoquinodimethane) upon solvation/desolvation of the crystallization solvent. The structure consists of two-dimensional layers with interstitial $\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ molecules located in the void spaces between the layers, which can be lost at room temperature to form a dried sample (Fig. 1). Interestingly, the original material can be regenerated upon exposure to $\mathbf{CH}_{2}\mathbf{Cl}_{2}$ vapors for $^{72\mathrm{~h~}}$ . Although the material exhibits a robust framework, this process produces a modification in the ordering/disordering of a pendant ligand that leads to a significant change in the magnetic ground state. Thus, the solvated compound possesses an antiferromagnetic ground state below $T_{\mathrm{N}}=75\ \mathrm{K},$ , which is altered to a ferromagnetic state with $T_{\mathrm{C}}\approx56~\mathrm{K}$ for the dried sample (Fig. 1). The magnetic change occurs as a result of slight structural fluctuations caused by the ordering/disordering of ligand orientation driven by the fix-andrelease of the $\mathbf{CH}_{2}\mathbf{Cl}_{2}$ molecules acting as a clip.
Reversible changes in the ligand structure may also occur in the magnetic MOF [Cu(F-pymo) ${}_{2}\big(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\big)_{1.25}\big]_{n}$ $\mathrm{\Delta}\mathrm{\cdot}\mathrm{\pymo}=5$ -fluoropyrimidin-2-olate) upon insertion of gas molecules in the pores. This compound, reported by Navarro, Parra, Sironi, and co-workers71 has helical channels of ca. 2.9 Å diameter which are filled with water molecules that can be removed upon heating (Fig. 2). The activated solid behaves as a molecular sieve and preferentially adsorbs $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ over ${\bf N}_{2}$ , in addition to $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ . The as-synthesized solid shows antiferromagnetic interactions and it orders as a canted antiferromagnet below $T_{\mathrm{N}}=24~\mathrm{K}$ . Such temperature is reduced to $22\mathrm{~K~}$ upon activation. Interestingly, the ordering temperature is increased to $29\mathrm{~\bf~K~}$ when the material is loaded with $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ (Fig. 2), presumably due to the structural perturbation exerted by the $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ guests, which affect the exchange interaction pathways through the ligands.
Changes affecting the coordination environment. Most of the known examples involving this kind of changes are related with the sorption/desorption of solvent molecules, most frequently water. In a first case an increase in the coordination number takes place when a solvent molecule is added.72 We can mention as example the three-dimensional coordination polymer $[\mathbf{Co}_{3}(\mathbf{L})_{2}(\mathbf{BTEC})]$ $\mathbf{[HL\=\3,5}$ -di(imidazol-1-yl)benzoic acid, $\mathbf{H_{4}B T E C}\ =\ 1,2,4,5\ ,$ -benzenetetracarboxylic acid] reported by Su et al.73 This MOF has 1D open channels (Fig. 3) that show selective adsorption of water molecules over ${\bf N}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{OH}$ , and $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{OH}$ . One of the $\mathbf{Co}^{\mathbf{\Pi}^{\mathrm{{II}}}}$ centres provides an open coordination site for the water molecule. Thus, upon exposure to air or water vapour or through the immersion into water at room temperature for several hours, the coordination geometry of this $\mathbf{Co}^{\mathrm{{II}}}$ centre changes from square pyramid to octahedron, producing a change in colour from dark-violet to red. Additional water molecules are located in the pores, resulting in the compound $\big[\mathrm{CO}_{3}\big(\mathrm{L}\big)_{2}\big(\mathrm{BTEC}\big)\big(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\big)_{2}\big]\cdot2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ . This dynamic process also alters the dimensions of the pores, which are expanded as a consequence of the sorption of water. The adsorption of water molecules produces some modifications in the ferromagnetic coupling between the $\mathbf{Co}^{\mathbf{\Pi}^{\mathrm{{II}}}}$ ions within the trinuclear unit, which are promoted through the carboxylate groups. The decrease in magnetic coupling J upon hydration is consistent with the increase in the Co–Co distance, whereas the increase in D is due to the change of geometry.
A second case involves an exchange of guest molecules by adsorbed molecules which also produces the elimination of a coordinated solvent molecule, thus resulting in the decrease in the coordination number of the metal centre. As example we can mention the ferromagnetic porous coordination polymer $\mathrm{{Cu_{3}}[\mathrm{{W(CN)_{8}}]_{2}(p y r i m i d i n e)_{2}{\cdot}8H_{2}O}}$ reported by Ohkoshi et al.74 In this MOF the magnetic properties can be tuned by the presence of $n$ -propanol vapors (Fig. 4). This heterometallic coordination polymer is composed of alternated Cu and W ions linked by cyanide groups. The two independent $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ ions present a pseudo-octahedral geometry with the coordination sphere completed either by pyrimidine ligands or water molecules. The two types of Cu centres result in two different Cu–NC–W exchange couplings, one ferromagnetic and one antiferromagnetic. The exposure of this MOF to vapours of $n$ -propanol produces an exchange of the solvent molecules, yielding a material of formula $\mathrm{{Cu}_{3}[W(C N)_{8}]_{2}(p y r i m i d i n e)_{2}\cdot3/2P r O H\cdot9/4H_{2}O,}$ where the noncoordinated water molecules have been replaced by propanol molecules. More interesting, however, is the elimination of one of the Cu-coordinated water molecules, yielding two distinct geometries: one Cu atom is 5-coordinated square pyramidal and the other Cu atom is a 6-coordinated pseudo-octahedron. This change of coordination of one Cu centre produces a change in the Cu–NC–W antiferromagnetic exchange coupling, whereas the ferromagnetic exchange coupling involving the 6-coordinate Cu centre remains the same. Thus, spontaneous magnetization is shifted from $T_{\mathrm{C}}=9.5\ \mathrm{K}$ to $T_{\mathrm{C}}=12.0~\mathrm{K}$ , and an increase in the coercive field $\mathrm{~from~}30\mathrm{~G~}$ to 1000 G) is observed.
Another example of dynamic MOF involving exchange of molecules at the coordination site is provided by the series $\mathrm{\big[Cu_{2}M(t z d c)_{2}(H_{2}O)_{2}\big]\cdot2H_{2}O}$ $[\mathbf{M}=\mathbf{F}\mathbf{e}^{2+}$ or $\ensuremath{\mathbf{M}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{n}}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{tzdc}^{3-}=1,2,3\cdot$ triazole-4,5-dicarboxylate] reported by Chen and co-workers.75 These two compounds contain microporous layers with water molecules allocated in the pores. The tzdc3 ligand acts as a $\upmu_{3}$ -bridge between three metals and provides the magnetic exchange pathway of the system. These materials release both the coordinated and the non-coordinated water molecules upon heating, a process that is accompanied by a change in the octahedral coordination sphere of the $\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}/\mathrm{Mn}^{2+}$ atoms from $\mathbf{O}_{6}$ to $\mathbf{O}_{4}\mathbf{N}_{2}$ . This modification produces a reversible change in the magnetism, in which the ferrimagnetic behaviour present in the original compound is suppressed (Fig. 5).
A similar situation is encountered in other coordination polymers based on cyanide or thiocyanide ligands, where the elimination of coordinated $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ molecules provokes a topochemical reaction with change of the coordination sphere from $\mathbf{N}_{4}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ to $\mathbf{N}_{6}$ with an increase in dimensionality from 1D to $\ensuremath{2\mathrm{D}}^{76,77}$ or from $\mathbf{N}_{3}\mathbf{N}^{\prime}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ to $\mathbf{N_{4}N^{\prime}}$ and $\mathbf{N}_{3}\mathbf{N}^{\prime78}$ with a reversible variation in $T_{\mathrm{c}}$ .
A third case involves the change in the coordination site from octahedral to tetrahedral, a situation that has been extensively encountered. An example has been reported by Zhang et al.79 in the chiral one-dimensional porous coordination polymer $\mathrm{\small{[Fe^{HI}(T p)(C N)_{3}]_{4}F e^{H}(H_{2}O)_{2}F e^{H}}}$ $\mathrm{Tp}\ =$ hydrotris(pyrazoly)- borate). This compound behaves as a metamagnet at $2~\mathrm{K},$ with a field-induced transition from an antiferromagnetic to a ferrimagnetic state. It is formed by $[\mathrm{Fe^{\mathrm{m}}(\mathrm{Tp)(\mathrm{CN)}_{3}]^{-}}}$ units that act as bidentate ligands through two of its three cyanide groups in cis positions, which coordinate the $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{II}}$ centres thus forming bimetallic double chains. Each $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{II}}$ acts as bridge between four $[\mathrm{Fe^{m}(T p)(C N)}_{3}]^{-}$ units and two water molecules are coordinated to alternated centres, resulting in two different coordination modes for the $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{II}}$ , octahedral and tetrahedral. Upon exposure to vapours of water and acetonitrile, the solvent molecules not only are sorbed in the voids, but also are inserted into some of the Fe–CN bonds causing a rupture of the chain and the formation of hexanuclear clusters of formula $\mathrm{[\{Fe^{HI}(T p)(C N)_{3}\}_{4}\{F e^{H}(M e C N)(H_{2}O)_{2}\}_{2}]\cdot10H_{2}O\cdot}$ 2MeCN, in which the tetrahedral $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{II}}$ sites have increased their coordination to octahedral. These hexanuclear units are antiferromagnetic and do not exhibit long range order. They are linked to each other to form a chain through hydrogen bonding interactions of the terminal $\mathbf{CN}^{-}$ on the $\mathrm{[Fe(Tp)(CN)_{3}]^{-}}$ moiety and the weakly coordinated solvent molecules on $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{II}}$ . Hence, upon solvation, the compound changes its magnetism from a metamagnet to an antiferromagnet, a process which is reversible upon heating at $150~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ .
A related example has recently been reported by Ohkoshi, Hashimoto, and co-workers.80 The compound is a Prussianblue analogue of formula $\mathrm{Co}[\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{CN})_{6}]_{2/3}{\cdot}z\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ . This pink solid is a ferromagnet with a $T_{\mathrm{C}}$ of $28\mathrm{~K~}$ that reversibly transforms into a blue antiferromagnet with a $T_{\mathrm{C}}$ of $22\mathrm{~K~}$ on dehydration (Fig. 6). This magnetic switching is ascribed to the adsorption and desorption of water molecules at the $\mathbf{Co}^{\mathrm{{II}}}$ centres. Such processes change the coordination geometry at $\mathbf{Co}^{\mathrm{{II}}}$ from pseudo-octahedral to pseudo-tetrahedral, thus modulating the magnetic interactions from ferromagnetic coupling in the hydrated solid to antiferromagnetic.
In the two previous examples the insertion of solvent molecules into the framework has produced a profound change in the structure and in the properties of the MOF. Analogue reactions using other co-ligands also show similar 3D–2D conversion due to the insertion of water molecules into M–NC bonds, producing a shift in $T_{\mathrm{C}}$ . Ohba, Kitagawa and co-workers81 have used $N,N\mathrm{.}$ -dimethylethylenediamine (NNdmen) to prepare porous $\mathrm{\small{[Mn(NNdmenH)][Cr(CN)_{6}]}},$ which shows a decrease in the ferrimagnetic ordering temperature from $60.4\mathrm{~K~}$ to $35.2\mathrm{~K~}$ upon hydration, whereas Inoue, Kurmoo and co-workers82 have used 1,2-propanediamine (pn) for the preparation of $\mathrm{\big[Mn(}r a c\mathrm{-pnH)][Cr(CN)_{6}]},$ which shows a decrease in the ferrimagnetic ordering temperature from $70~\mathrm{K}$ to $36~\mathrm{K}$ upon hydration. Although dehydration processes are quite common, the examples here presented are reversible and occur without collapse of the structure.
A very interesting example that combines mainly all types of changes described above was recently reported by Pinkowicz, Sieklucka and co-workers.83 $\begin{array}{r l}&{\{[\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}^{\mathrm{H}}(\mathbf{p}\mathbf{y}\mathbf{d}\mathbf{z})(\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O})_{2}][\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}^{\mathrm{H}}(\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O})_{2}][\mathbf{N}\mathbf{b}^{\mathrm{\mathbf{N}}}(\mathbf{C}\mathbf{N})_{8}].}\end{array}$ $2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\uprangle_{n}$ (pydz $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}$ pyridazine) is a 3D coordination polymer with a cyanido-bridged $\mathbf{Mn}_{2}\mathbf{N}\mathbf{b}$ skeleton consisting of a Mn–NC–Nb squaregrid cross-linked at ${\mathbf{Nb}}^{\mathbf{N}}$ centers by Mn–NC–Nb ladders. The only terminal CN ligand of the $\mathrm{\big[Nb(CN)_{8}\big]}$ moiety is involved in a hydrogen bond with one of the coordinated $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ molecules, whereas the noncoordinating nitrogen atom of the pyridazine ligand hydrogen bonds the other coordinated $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ molecule. Four water molecules per formula unit can be removed upon a few hours of dehydration, resulting in $\{[\mathbf{M}\boldsymbol{\mathrm{n}}^{\mathrm{H}}(\mathrm{pyd}\mathbf{z})(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathbf{O})][\mathbf{M}\boldsymbol{\mathrm{n}}^{\mathrm{H}}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathbf{O})][\mathbf{N}\mathbf{b}^{\mathrm{IV}}(\mathbf{C}\mathbf{N})_{8}]\}_{n}$ The loss of water permits a double topochemical reaction in which the terminal cyanide ligand and the noncoordinating nitrogen atom of the pydz ligand (which previously hydrogen bonded the water molecules) coordinate to Mn centres. In addition, the bond between the pydz ligand and Mn breaks. This change produces a change in the coordination spheres of the Mn centres from the original octahedral $\mathbf{N}_{3}\mathbf{N}^{\prime}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ and $\mathbf{N}_{4}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ to square-pyramidal geometry ${\bf N}_{4}\mathrm{O}$ and octahedral $\mathrm{\bfN}_{4}\mathrm{\bfN}^{\prime}\mathrm{\bfO}$ respectively. Further dehydration produces the subsequent formation of the anhydrous phase $\{[\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}^{\mathrm{H}}{}_{2}(\mathbf{p}\mathbf{y}\mathbf{d}\mathbf{z})][\mathbf{N}\mathbf{b}^{\mathrm{IV}}(\mathbf{CN})_{8}]\}_{n},$ affecting only the coordination spheres of both Mn atoms (which are tetrahedral $\mathbf{N}_{4}$ and trigonal-bipyramidal $\mathbf{N_{4}N^{\prime}}$ ). Interestingly, this two-step process is fully reversible. These structural changes cause an increase in the ordering temperature of the coordination polymer
$43\mathrm{K}\rightarrow68\mathrm{K}\rightarrow100\mathrm{K}$ ) due to the increase in the connectivity of the magnetic centres and the reduced coordination numbers caused by the dehydration process (Fig. 7).
No significant structural changes. Although they might not be considered ‘‘dynamic MOFs’’, there are also microporous framework materials where the guest sorption does not affect either the ligand or the coordination environment, but modification of the magnetic properties occurs.84–94 One example is the family of porous magnets $[\mathbf{M}_{3}(\mathbf{HCOO})_{6}]_{\mathrm{:}}$ where $\mathbf{M}=\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}$ , Fe, Co, or Ni,89–94 recently discovered independently by three groups, which consists of a diamond net combining octahedral and tetrahedral nodes with the pores occupied by a great variety of solvent molecules, which can be removed upon heating. Despite the lack of changes in the framework upon guest sorption, these compounds exhibit ferromagnetic ordering with a guestdependent critical temperature, which might be due to the modification of the host–guest hydrogen-bonding network.
2.1.2. Spin crossover frameworks. Dynamic porous materials based on spin-crossover complexes are very numerous.95 In fact, all spin crossover complexes could be considered as dynamic materials due to the expansion/shrinkage of the lattice generated by this phenomenon. The origin of these dynamics arises from the variation in the number of electrons in bonding and anti-bonding orbitals caused by the spin transition that modifies the metal– ligand distances, leading to an expansion/contraction of the framework. Temperature, light or pressure are common physical stimuli that produce this transition, but MOFs based on these molecular complexes can also exhibit magnetic switching via a chemical stimulus. This aspect has only been recently developed, although examples of spin-crossover MOFs were already known almost 20 years ago.96 Typically, the high sensitivity of these porous materials is evidenced by the modification of the transition temperature upon physisorption of specific molecules within the porous framework97–103 or even upon oxidative addition reactions following gas sorption at metal sites that are far from the magnetic centres.104,105 Here we will focus on the two main families of SCO porous MOFs.
Guest modulated spin-crossover. One of the first examples of modulation by guest sorption of the spin-crossover properties in porous materials was shown by Kepert and co-workers.97 The nanoporous metal–organic framework $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}(\mathrm{azpy})_{4}(\mathrm{NCS})_{4}{\cdot}(\mathrm{guest})$ $(\mathrm{azpy}=t r a n s{-}4,4^{\prime}$ -azopyridine) displays reversible uptake and release of guest molecules (MeOH, EtOH, and $\scriptstyle n-\mathrm{PrOH}.$ ), which form hydrogen bonds with the framework. The framework structure displays a considerable flexibility with the guest uptake and release (Fig. 8), causing substantial changes in the local geometry around the $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{II}}$ centres. Thus, the sorbed phases undergo ‘‘half-spin’’ crossovers, whereas the desorbed phases show no switching property. In the synthetic procedure, the azopyridine ligand can be replaced by different bis-pyridyl ligands producing the most extensive class of porous SCO materials,98–101 in which the desorption/adsorption of guest molecules into the 1-D channels produces the variation in SCO temperature as a consequence of the structural transformations associated with the guest sorption.
More recently, the effects of guest molecules in pillared Hofmann systems $\{\mathrm{Fe(pz)[{M(CN)_{4}]}\}(\mathbf{M}=\mathbf{Ni},^{102}\mathbf{Pt}^{103})}$ have been reported (Fig. 9a). These systems exhibit hysteretic spin-crossover under ambient conditions. In two independent studies, Ohba et al. and Southon et al. have shown the stabilization of either the high spin (HS) or the low spin (LS) states of the porous framework upon sorption of guest molecules, achieving both guest-induced SCO and SCO-induced guest-exchange properties (Fig. 9b).
Spin-crossover induced by a change in the oxidation state of the metal. An interesting example in which a thermal treatment of a MOF induces a change in the oxidation state of the metal, which leads to a controlled modulation of the magnetic properties, has been found in the layered compound $\mathrm{[Co^{III}L a^{I I I}-(n o t p)(H_{2}O)_{4}]}{\cdot}n\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ [where $\left(\mathrm{notp}\right)^{6-}$ is the hexaanion of the 1,4,7-triazacyclononane1,4,7-triyl-tris(methylenephosphonic acid]. This heterometallic coordination polymer has been reported by Bao et $a l.^{106}$ The diamagnetic compound is formed by binding an octahedral $\mathrm{Co}^{\mathrm{III}}(\mathrm{notp})^{3-}$ unit to $\mathrm{La}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{4}^{3+}$ through phosphonate oxygen atoms. Upon heating, both the coordinated and non-coordinated water molecules can be removed, producing a change in the oxidation state of the cobalt (from $\mathbf{Co}^{\mathrm{{III}}}$ to $\mathrm{Co}^{\mathrm{II}}$ (Fig. 10). As a result, the spin state of the compound changes from a diamagnetic lowspin state (for $\mathrm{Co}^{\mathrm{III}}$ ) to a paramagnetic high-spin state (for $\mathrm{Co}^{\mathrm{II}}$ ). This process is reversible on rehydration in air. The electron transfer is caused by the gradual release of the coordinated water molecules around the La atom. A combination of UV/Vis and EXAFS spectroscopies and DFT calculations was used to elucidate the origin of the change of the electronic structure of the cobalt centre, and it was found that the four vacant sites of the lanthanide ion upon removal of the water molecules are refilled by the neighbouring oxygen atoms of the adjacent Co(notp) moieties. This causes an increase in the $\scriptstyle\mathbf{Co-O}$ bond lengths and a decrease in the Co–N bond lengths, changing ligand-field strength mediating the spin state of the Co ions.
Changes in the coordination environment of the SCO centres. Another possibility for tuning the spin-crossover properties consists of the modification of the coordination sphere of the SCO centres. This approach has been employed by Aromı´, Gamez and co-workers, who have synthesized a linear coordination polymer of formula $\mathrm{\small{[Fe_{3}(d p y a t r i z)_{2}(C H_{3}C H_{2}C N)_{4}-}}$ $\mathrm{\big(BF_{4}\big)_{2}\big]\big(B F_{4}\big)_{4}\cdot4C H_{3}C H_{2}C N}$ with one-dimensional channels (Fig. 11a).107 These channels provide a path for the exchange of the coordinated propionitrile ligands. Thus, replacement with acetonitrile or 1-propanol was found to take place upon immersion into the corresponding solvent, with a decrease in the transition temperature from $300\mathrm{K}$ to $273\mathrm{K}$ (acetonitrile) or suppression of the spin-crossover properties, but maintaining the overall crystal structure (Fig. 11b). However, replacement with water molecules from the atmosphere results in a structural change (reversible upon immersion into propionitrile), most likely due to the different hydrogen bonding capabilities of the different ligands. This transformation involves suppression of the spin-crossover properties, presumably due to a reduction of the ligand field around the $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{II}}$ .
The possibility of synthesizing Hofmann chlatrates with Pt open metal sites permits us to tune the transition temperature of this type of dynamic porous coordination polymers by oxidative addition of halogens. Thus, upon exposure of $\{\mathrm{Fe(pz)[Pt(CN)_{4}]}\}$ to dihalogens, an associative oxidation of $\mathbf{Pt}^{\mathrm{{II}}}$ to $\mathrm{Pt}^{\mathrm{IV}}$ occurs with a reduction of the dihalogen to the corresponding halide resulting in the formation of $\{\mathrm{Fe(pz)}[\mathrm{Pt(CN)}_{4}(\mathrm{X})_{p}]\}$ $[\mathrm{X}=\mathrm{Cl}^{-}~(p=1),\mathrm{Br}^{-}~(p=1),\mathrm{I}^{-}~(0<p<1)]$ .104,105 This oxidative addition to the Pt centres modifies the $\upsigma$ -donor capability of the nitrogen atom of the $\mathrm{Pt-CN}$ group, provoking a change in $T_{\mathrm{C}},$ . Thus, the more electronegative X induces a decrease in the ligand field, and so $T_{\mathrm{C}}$ is decreased.
## 2.2. Non-porous MOFs
As seen in the previous section, porous coordination polymers have provided many examples in which the physisorption of guest molecules provoke subtle (or even profound) changes in the framework that affect their magnetic properties. However, the preparation of porous magnetic materials is not easy because magnetic coupling between spin carriers is weak in the porous network. Very recently we have shown that magnetic properties can also be tuned through gas uptake in the absence of pores.109 Since there is no possibility of a ‘‘guest molecule’’ being accommodated within pores, the gas molecules react with the material being therefore incorporated as part of the network. This results in significant chemical changes and atomic motions within the crystalline solid.
Crystalline $[\mathrm{Cu(pyim)(Cl)(MeOH)}]$ $\mathrm{(pyimH}\ =\ 2\cdot$ (imidazol-2-yl)- pyridine) displays a two-step irreversible–reversible chemisorption process, which causes substantial changes in the geometry and connectivity between the metal centres (Fig. 13), leading to three different compounds that exhibit distinct magnetic properties (Fig. 14). Thus, the as-synthesized material forms antiferromagnetic chains of pentacoordinated square pyramidal Cu centres. Upon chemisorption of 3 equivalents of HCl molecules the resultant ionic solid, $\mathrm{(H_{2}p y i m H)[C u C l_{4}]}.$ presents ferromagnetic interactions between the distorted tetrahedral $\mathrm{[CuCl_{4}]^{2-}}$ anions. Although this process is not fully reversible, this compound is able to extrude 2 equivalents of HCl yielding the molecular compound cis- $\mathrm{\cdot}\mathrm{[cuCl_{2}}$ - (pyimH)] where the square planar Cu centres show antiferromagnetic interactions. The magneto-structural switching of these materials arises from the presence of labile copper(II) centres within the coordination network, which are capable of reacting with gaseous HCl or releasing HCl in a cyclic manner without loss of effectiveness. Importantly, these non-porous frameworks display a considerable flexibility with the guest uptake and release, which do not disrupt the crystallinity of the compounds. The structural analysis shows that three equivalents of HCl molecules are inserted into all $\mathrm{{Cu-N}}$ bonds requiring rupture of the covalent bond of the gaseous molecules (H–Cl) and of the coordination bonds of the polymeric compound in order that new N–H and Cu–Cl bonds can be formed. Thus, the coordination sphere of the Cu centres changes from three Cu–N, one $\mathbf{Cu-Cl}$ and one $\mathrm{{Cu-O}}$ bonds in the square pyramidal geometry to four Cu–Cl bonds in a distorted tetrahedral arrangement. The extrusion of HCl implies the elimination of two equivalents of HCl gas molecules resulting in the formation of a square planar Cu molecular complex with two Cu–N and two Cu–Cl bonds. Although the magnetic changes caused by the structural transformations in these compounds are only observable in the low temperature region (below 10 K) except for the pristine material, the substantial changes in coordination environments due to the sorption/desorption processes have permitted the use of electron paramagnetic resonance (EPR) spectroscopy to monitor the reaction at room temperature due to its high sensitivity to the coordination environment of the metal centres.
Another possibility for tuning the magnetic properties in nonporous coordination polymers is by anion exchange. We have recently shown that a 3D coordination polymer, $\mathrm{[Cu(btix)}_{2}(\mathbf{BF}_{4})_{2}]_{n}$ (btix $\mathbf{\Psi}=1$ ,4-bis(triazol-1-ylmethyl)benzene) can reversibly exchange the coordinated $\mathrm{BF_{4}}^{-}$ anions by different anions with retention $\left(\mathrm{PF_{6}}^{-}$ , $\mathrm{ClO_{4}}^{-})$ or modification $\left(\mathrm{NO_{3}}^{-},\mathrm{Cl}^{-}\right)$ of the crystal structure, but maintaining the organic ligand as the only linker between the metal centers.110 However, an irreversible exchange occurs with ${\mathbf{N_{3}}}^{-}$ anions to generate a new coordination polymer, $\begin{array}{r}{[\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{btix})(\mathbf{N}_{3})_{2}]_{n},}\end{array}$ with a totally different connectivity between the $\mathrm{{Cu}^{\mathrm{{II}}}}$ centres, which now involves both the organic and the inorganic ligands. During this process a drastic rearrangement of the network occurs due to the exchange of the $\mathrm{BF_{4}}^{-}$ anion by a ${\mathbf{N_{3}}}^{-}$ anion, accompanied by loss of one coordinated btix ligand, which is transferred to the aqueous solution. As expected, this structural transformation is accompanied with a change in the magnetic properties, which switches from antiferromagnetic in the pristine material to ferromagnetic in the azide exchanged system.
## 3. Physically-driven dynamic MOFs
Dynamic magnetic MOFs driven by a physical stimulus are scarce. A major source of such materials uses thermal heating to produce the structural change in the MOF. In this sense, some of the materials reported in the previous section are also thermally-driven, although the uptake/release of a physisorbed or a chemisorbed molecule is common to all of them. Furthermore, in these cases rigid ligands have been used, in such a way that a chemical stimulus has been able to cause minor changes in the conformation of the bridging ligands. In this section we will focus exclusively on those coordination polymers in which a physical stimulus is able to induce a dynamic change in the bridging ligands that are linking the metal ions. Two relevant examples will illustrate this possibility. In the first one, we will take advantage of the ability of some flexible ligands to undergo a syn–anti isomerism upon heating. In the second one, we will use the ability of the non-symmetric cyanide ligand to undergo a rotation induced by pressure when it is connecting $\mathrm{Fe}^{\mathrm{II}}$ to $\mathrm{Cr}^{\mathrm{III}}$ in a Prussian blue analogue.
## 3.1. Syn–anti isomerism upon heating
Very recently, we have reported a family of dynamic coordination polymers with the flexible ligand 1,4-bis(triazol-1- ylmethyl)benzene (btix) where the ligand adopts a syn conformation, $\left\{\left[\mathbf{M}(s y n\mathbf{-btix})_{2}(\mathbf{OH}_{2})_{2}\right]\cdot2\mathbf{NO}_{3}\cdot2\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}\right\}_{n}$ $[\mathbf{M_{\alpha}}=\mathbf{C_{0}}$ , Zn].111 This framework consists of non-interpenetrated 1D cationic chains with large cavities that are partially filled with anions and solvent molecules. Upon heating, the chain compounds are able to extrude both coordinated and non-bonded water molecules, but also induces a conformational change in the flexible btix ligands from syn-conformation to anti-conformation, leading to a material of formula $\{[\mathbf{M}(a n t i\mathbf{-}\mathbf{b}\mathrm{tix})_{2}(\mathbf{NO}_{3})_{2}]\}_{n;\l}$ which forms a 2D neutral grid with (4,4) topology (Fig. 15). This solid-state transformation requires a rearrangement in the metal environment and implies the cleavage of coordination bonds between $\mathbf{M}^{\mathrm{II}}$ ions and btix ligands, followed by the rotation of btix ligands through the methylene groups to adopt the anti conformation and subsequent re-formation of $\mathbf{M}{\cdot}\mathbf{N}$ bonds. Additionally, a ligand substitution takes place since the loss of coordinated water molecules is followed by coordination of the ${\mathrm{NO_{3}}}^{-}$ anions.
This structural transformation has been monitored through the use of magnetic techniques as it provokes changes in the $\mathbf{Co}^{\mathbf{II}}$ coordination sphere upon transformation of the chains to the layered structure, and this transformation has been followed without detecting an intermediate in the reaction (Fig. 16). Hence, in this work it has been shown the possibility of using a magnetic technique as the EPR to monitor the structural changes induced in a dynamic MOF.
